  "response": "Yes, response-hook return values are covered at the dispatch level. In `tests/test_hooks.py::test_hooks`, a hook returning `value[1:]` is tested both as a single callable (`\"Data\"` becomes `\"ata\"`) and in a callable list (`\"Data\"` becomes `\"ta\"` after two transforming hooks with an intervening `None`-returning hook) [Data: Sources (74)].\n\nThe implementation in `src/requests/hooks.py::dispatch_hook` confirms the tested behavior: each hook receives the current data, and a non-`None` return value replaces it; a `None` return leaves the current value unchanged [Data: Sources (43)]. The same test therefore covers both transformation and non-transformation return paths.\n\nHowever, the supplied snapshot does not show `Session.send` or a test that registers a response hook during `Session.send` and verifies which response later steps use. The pickle test only uses `default_hooks()`, whose response-hook list is empty, so it does not establish end-to-end `Session.send` behavior [Data: Sources (91)]. It is therefore insufficient to conclude from these tests that every registered callable necessarily returns the final `Response` used by subsequent `Session.send` steps.",
  "score": 92,
  "follow_up_queries": [
    "Where does `Session.send` call `dispatch_hook`, and does it assign the returned value back to the response?",
    "Is there an integration test with multiple response hooks, including a hook that returns `None` and one that returns a replacement `Response`?"
  ]
}
